ism": "UV photoredox reduction and homologation of cyanide-derived intermediates, followed by hydrolysis, furnishes glycolaldehyde.", "reasoning": "UV cyanosulfidic chemistry directly links cyanide feedstocks to glycolaldehyde under anoxic surface conditions and is one of the strongest experimental surface routes to this C2 hydroxyaldehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Partial reduction of glyoxal to glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["FeS", "NiS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-420K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "FeS/NiS mineral surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "H2-rich subseafloor fluids; mildly alkaline conditions"}, "mechanism": "Surface-mediated hydrogenation reduces one aldehyde of glyoxal to the corresponding primary alcohol.", "reasoning": "Reducing one carbonyl of glyoxal is chemically simpler than building a new C-C bond and is compatible with H2-rich FeS/NiS settings, giving a conservative hydrothermal entry into the glycolaldehyde hub."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Hydrothermal CO homologation to glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["awaruite (Ni3Fe)", "greigite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "reduced Fe-Ni alloy and sulfide surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "CO-rich alkaline vent fluids with strong redox gradients"}, "mechanism": "Adsorbed CO-derived formyl/carbonyl fragments undergo hydrogenation and C-C coupling on reduced mineral surfaces, releasing glycolaldehyde.", "reasoning": "FTT-like CO homologation on reduced Fe-Ni minerals can in principle furnish oxygenated C2 products. Direct glycolaldehyde release remains less secure experimentally than the surface routes, so this branch is included as a plausible but more speculative hydrothermal alternative."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Strecker synthesis of serine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_006", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "290-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "concentrated prebiotic pools or mineral interfaces; evaporation concentration; pH 6-8"}, "mechanism": "Imine formation from glycolaldehyde and ammonia is followed by cyanide addition to form the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Glycolaldehyde already contains the carbon skeleton required for serine, so Strecker capture is a short route to the correct beta-hydroxylated aminonitrile. Concentrated prebiotic pools can accumulate ammonia and cyanide alongside the aldehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Direct hydrolysis of serine nitrile to serine", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Fe2+-rich clay interfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Fe2+-rich mineral surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "near-boiling geothermal cycling with repeated heating-cooling"}, "mechanism": "Net nitrile hydration and hydrolysis yields the free amino acid.", "reasoning": "Aminonitriles are masked amino acids. Repeated near-boiling pool cycles and mineral-assisted hydration can carry serine nitrile to serine, although the beta-hydroxylated product remains vulnerable to side reactions."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Partial hydrolysis of serine nitrile to serinamide", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["silica", "montmorillonite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silica/clay mineral surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "shorter geothermal pulses; incomplete hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "Single-stage nitrile hydration gives the amide.", "reasoning": "Under milder or shorter-lived hydrolysis conditions, the nitrile can stop at the amide stage. This creates a distinct reservoir that can be converted later under continued cycling."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolysis of serinamide to serine", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Fe2+-rich clay interfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Fe2+-rich mineral surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "extended aqueous residence during geothermal cycling"}, "mechanism": "Amide hydrolysis liberates the carboxylic acid.", "reasoning": "Amide hydrolysis is slower than nitrile hydration but becomes plausible in repeatedly heated aqueous pools, releasing free serine once enough residence time is available."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs type formation of 5-hydroxymethylhydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_006", "mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["carbonate-rich mineral interfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "carbonate-concentrating mineral surfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "concentrated ammonium carbonate/cyanide chemistry in prebiotic pools"}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin and imino intermediates trap carbamoyl equivalents and cyclize to the substituted hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Bucherer-Bergs-type chemistry offers an alternative protected precursor: the reactive glycolaldehyde carbon skeleton is trapped as a hydroxymethylhydantoin in carbonate-, ammonia-, and cyanide-rich pools."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrolysis of 5-hydroxymethylhydantoin to serine", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["clay mineral interfaces"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "clay-rich geothermal interfaces", "aqueous": true, "other": "extended hydrolysis under anoxic geothermal cycling"}, "mechanism": "Ring opening and carbamoyl hydrolysis liberate the corresponding amino acid.", "reasoning": "Hydantoins are classic masked amino