e chemistry represented separately in rxn_005 and rxn_006."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Partial hydrolysis of serine nitrile to serinamide", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "hydrating pool or mineral interface; first hydrolysis step favored before full acid formation"}, "mechanism": "Nitrile hydration converts serine nitrile to the corresponding amide, serinamide.", "reasoning": "The amide is the expected first hydrolysis product of an amino nitrile and is a likely kinetic bottleneck. Including serinamide explicitly gives a more conservative and mechanistically resolved route to serine."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolysis of serinamide to serine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-380K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "continued aqueous heating in geothermal cycling or long-residence pools"}, "mechanism": "Serinamide hydrolyzes to the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Once the amide is formed, further hydrolysis yields serine under sustained aqueous heating. This stepwise branch is often more realistic than assuming immediate full nitrile hydrolysis."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs-type formation of 5-hydroxymethylhydantoin from glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_006", "mol_005", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_007"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanide and ammonium carbonate-rich pool or evaporative pore network; CO2 and NH3 act through carbamate or carbonate speciation"}, "mechanism": "Glycolaldehyde enters a Bucherer-Bergs-type manifold: cyanide and ammonia generate the amino-nitrile chemistry, carbamylation by CO2-derived species follows, and ring closure yields 5-hydroxymethylhydantoin.", "reasoning": "Hydantoins are plausible protected reservoirs of amino-acid skeletons under cyanide and carbonate chemistry. This branch is slower and more condition-sensitive than direct Strecker synthesis but can preserve the serine framework in a less labile form."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Overall hydrolysis of 5-hydroxymethylhydantoin to serine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-390K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "net two-water hydrolysis and decarbamylation under prolonged heating"}, "mechanism": "Ring opening, ureide hydrolysis, and decarbamylation convert 5-hydroxymethylhydantoin to serine; this node encodes the overall transformation when the carbamylated amino-acid intermediate does not accumulate.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis to amino acids is chemically well precedented under sustained heating. This node is a coarse-grained overall transformation; the more explicit stepwise alternative through N-carbamylserine is given in rxn_009 and rxn_010."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Ring opening of 5-hydroxymethylhydantoin to N-carbamylserine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly alkaline or neutral aqueous conditions; partial hydrolysis before full decarbamylation"}, "mechanism": "Hydantoin ring opening by water gives the corresponding N-carbamyl amino acid, N-carbamylserine.", "reasoning": "N-carbamyl amino acids are the expected intermediates of hydantoin hydrolysis. Including N-carbamylserine explicitly captures the chemically orthodox stepwise route and avoids overcompressing the branch."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Hydrolysis and decarbamylation of N-carbamylserine to serine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-390K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "continued aqueous heating or geothermal cycling promotes carbamyl cleavage"}, "mechanism": "N-carbamylserine hydrolyzes and decarbamylates to serine, releasing carbon dioxide and ammonia.", "reasoning": "This step completes the hydantoin branch through a chemically orthodox intermediate. It is slower than direct Strecker-to-hydrolysis chemistry but can benefit from the greater persistence of hydantoin reservoirs in fluctuating prebiotic pools."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Strecker synthesis of glycine nitrile from formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_007"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "290-330K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "concentrated NH3 and HCN pool or pore network; formaldehyde serves as the aldehyde component"}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde reacts with ammonia to give an iminium or carbinolamine manifold that is trapped by cyanide, yielding aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "Glycine nitrile is the Strecker product of the simplest aldehyde and is a plausible aminonitrile reservoir. Including it creates an alternative serine entry that does not require prior formation of glycolaldehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Homologation of glycine nitrile with formaldehyde to serine nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm to pore-confined", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "