{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T17:32:29.895363", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Serine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.47\n- Feedback: The network is connected, contains enough nodes to define multiple routes, and the glycolaldehyde-centered strategy is conceptually coherent. However, network validity is strongly limited by the deterministic balance check: only 1 of 10 reactions is balanced as written, 3 are balanceable only with omitted coefficients, and 6 are explicit conservation violations. The most important fixes are: rxn_001 should be written as 2 CH2O -> glycolaldehyde; rxn_004 should be 2 CO + 2 H2 -> glycolaldehyde; rxn_005 should produce H2O; rxn_006 should be serine nitrile + 2 H2O -> serine + NH3; rxn_007 should consume H2O; rxn_008 should be serinamide + H2O -> serine + NH3; rxn_009 is chemically plausible but mis-specified and likely needs H2O as a product in a one-turnover Bucherer-Bergs form; rxn_010 should include 2 H2O and release CO2 + NH3 or be split through N-carbamylserine. Chemically, the strongest entry routes are rxn_001 and especially rxn_003; rxn_002 is not acceptable as written, and rxn_004 remains highly speculative. Environmental consistency is weakened because 'Biochemical' is not a defined prebiotic environment and transfers from surface or hydrothermal settings into concentration and hydrolysis pools are not justified. P6 is the best pathway, but the network is not approvable as written.\n- Issues: ['Conceptually reasonable glycolaldehyde -> serine nitrile -> serine route, but two of the three steps are mis-specified. rxn_005 is a Strecker step that should release H2O. rxn_006 is an unbalanced conservation violation; a chemically reasonable overall form is serine nitrile + 2 H2O -> serine + NH3. The formose entry is plausible on mineral-rich surfaces, but selectivity and transfer into the aqueous hydrolysis setting are not explained.', \"This is more coherent than P1 because amino nitrile hydrolysis normally proceeds through an amide. However rxn_005 is missing H2O as a product, rxn_007 is missing H2O as a reactant, and rxn_008 should consume H2O and release NH3. The overall route is plausible in a concentrated aqueous pool, but the network labels those steps only as 'Biochemical' and does not justify transfer from the surface source of glycolaldehyde.\", 'The entry step is unacceptable as written. rxn_002, HCN + H2S -> glycolaldehyde, is deterministically unbalanced and no simple missing small molecule fixes it. It also does not represent the actual cyanosulfidic photoredox chemistry, which requires a larger UV-driven redox network and realistic metal mediation. Downstream, rxn_005 should release H2O and rxn_006 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3.', 'The stepwise hydrolysis part is better than direct nitrile hydrolysis, but the pathway is still undermined by rxn_002. As written, the cyanosulfidic entry is not mass balanced and omits the actual photoredox network. Downstream corrections are also required: rxn_005 should produce H2O, rxn_007 should consume H2O, and rxn_008 should consume H2O and release NH3.', 'This is a relatively defensible entry route because rxn_003 is balanced and hydrothermal reduction of glyoxal to glycolaldehyde is chemically plausible with suitable mineral catalysts. The weaknesses are downstream: rxn_005 needs H2O as a product and rxn_006 needs 2 H2O as reactants plus NH3 as a product. The pathway also assumes export of glycolaldehyde from a hydrothermal setting into a concentrated aqueous pool without justification.', \"This is the strongest pathway in the network. rxn_003 is balanced and reasonably plausible under hydrothermal conditions with H2 and suitable mineral catalysts, and stepwise nitrile -> amide -> amino acid hydrolysis is more realistic than direct nitrile hydrolysis. However the written stoichiometry still fails: rxn_005 should produce H2O, rxn_007 should consume H2O, and rxn_008 should consume H2O and release NH3. The downstream environment also needs to be stated as a specific prebiotic aqueous setting rather than 'Biochemical'.\", 'Even after balancing rxn_004 as 2 CO + 2 H2 -> glycolaldehyde, the entry chemistry remains highly speculative. Direct hydrothermal CO homologation to glycolaldehyde is not well established and would require strong catalytic justification. Downstream, rxn_005 should release H2O and rxn_006 should consume 2 H2O and release NH3.', 'The amide-mediated hydrolysis sequence is sensible, but the pathway still depends on a weak entry reaction. rxn_004 is only a speculative hydrothermal CO homologation proposal even when written with the correct 2 CO + 2 H2 stoichiometry. In addition, rxn_005 should produce H2O, rxn_007 should consume H2O, and rxn_008 should consume H2O and release NH3.', 'The hydantoin reservoir concept is coherent and addresses precursor stability, but the reaction specification is poor. rxn_009 is only formally balanceable through implausibly large coefficients, which strongly suggests a mis-specified Bucherer-Bergs step; the chemically meaningful one-turnover version likely needs H2O as a product. rxn_010 is an unbalanced conservation violation and should include water plus release of CO2 and NH3, or be split through an N-carbamylserine intermediate.', 'This combines the weakest entry step with a mis-specified hydantoin branch. rxn_002 is not mass balanced and does not represent actual cyanosulfidic photoredox synthesis of glycolaldehyde. rxn_009 likely needs H2O as a product in a chemically sensible Bucherer-Bergs formulation, and rxn_010 should include 2 H2O and release CO2 plus NH3 or proceed through an N-carbamylserine intermediate.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.78\n- Feedback: Connected, internally consistent network with no conservation violations in the provided RDKit check; rxn_001, rxn_004, and rxn_008 just need explicit coefficients. Strengths are the coherent protected-precursor routes to serine, especially the glycolaldehyde Strecker/serinamide branch and the hydantoin/N-carbamylserine branch. Main weaknesses are that the generic Biochemical label is not a concrete environment, catalytic and selectivity requirements are under-specified for rxn_001 and rxn_002, rxn_007 needs carbonate/cyanide speciation defined, and rxn_012 is a much weaker and more speculative homologation than the rest of the network. Overall this is a credible but not high-confidence serine network and it does not meet the whole-network approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['Coherent route once glycolaldehyde is available. rxn_003 is plausible Strecker chemistry, but rxn_001 is under-specified and usually needs mineral or basic catalysis and should be written 2 CH2O -> C2H4O2. rxn_004 is a valid net hydrolysis only with 2 H2O and likely proceeds stepwise under longer-lived acidic, alkaline, or mineral-catalyzed conditions.', 'The protected hydantoin manifold is strategically sensible, but rxn_007 needs better reagent speciation because Bucherer-Bergs chemistry is really cyanide plus ammonium carbonate or bicarbonate chemistry, not just free HCN and CO2. rxn_008 is also only a net hydrolysis and should include 2 H2O and sufficiently forcing conditions.', 'The route is chemically coherent if glycolaldehyde can be generated hydrothermally, but rxn_002 depends on selective partial reduction of glyoxal under catalyst-controlled conditions. Movement from a hydrothermal source into a cooler HCN and NH3 rich setting is plausible but only implicit, and rxn_004 remains a condensed hydrolysis step.', 'Stepwise hydrolysis improves the endgame, but confidence is still capped by rxn_002, which needs explicit metal or sulfide catalysis and a transport or quench scenario before Strecker chemistry. Once glycolaldehyde is present, the rest of the pathway is reasonable.', 'This pathway stacks several condition-sensitive steps: selective hydrothermal reduction, Bucherer-Bergs-type hydantoin formation with correct cyanide and carbonate speciation, and a forcing final hydrolysis. It is conceivable, but the environmental definition is too vague for high confidence.', 'Stepwise hydantoin opening is more realistic than direct hydantoin-to-serine conversion, but the pathway still relies on under-specified hydrothermal reduction and on later aqueous conditions that must support carbonate and cyanide chemistry and N-carbamylserine hydrolysis.', 'rxn_011 is solid, but rxn_012 is the weak link. Direct homologation of glycine nitrile with formaldehyde to serine nitrile is chemically imaginable only under specific deprotonating or catalytic conditions and is much less established than using glycolaldehyde directly. Final hydrolysis also needs 2 H2O in the written stoichiometry.', 'Using serinamide makes the hydrolysis sequence more realistic, but the branch is still limited by the speculative C-C bond-forming rxn_012. Without stronger support for that homologation, this route is distinctly weaker than the glycolaldehyde-based branches.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Serine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network is intentionally surface-centered because the strongest prebiotic routes to serine rely on cyanide chemistry, carbonate speciation, and wet-dry concentration in evaporitic or geothermal pools. A common C2 hub, glycolaldehyde, is supplied either by alkaline formaldehyde dimerization or by Fe-Ni-sulfide-assisted partial reduction of glyoxal in H2-bearing geothermal settings. From glycolaldehyde, five downstream branches reach serine: one-pot Strecker plus direct hydrolysis; one-pot Strecker plus amide-mediated hydrolysis; stepwise imine formation plus cyanide addition plus direct hydrolysis; the same stepwise Strecker route plus amide-mediated hydrolysis; and a Bucherer-Bergs hydantoin route via N-carbamylserine. These two entry routes combined with five downstream branches provide 10 connected pathways.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanedial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxal\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"HCN\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethan-1-imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde imine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H8N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(hydroxymethyl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-carbamylserine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH5NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ammonium hydrogen carbonate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonium bicarbonate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde dimerization to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"brucite\", \"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"brucite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline evaporitic pool or tidal-flat film; wet-dry cycles; borate-rich microenvironments help retain the C2 aldose and limit runaway formose chemistry\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-catalyzed initial formose step in which two formaldehyde equivalents couple to glycolaldehyde; borate complexes the product diol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This gives the first C-C bond from a very simple C1 feedstock. The step is known to be low-selectivity if the full formose cascade runs unchecked, so the network treats it as a low-conversion initiation step under alkaline, mineral-buffered conditions rather than as bulk sugar synthesis.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Selective reduction of glyoxal to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"FeS\", \"NiS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe-Ni sulfides\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"subaerial geothermal field or volcanic hot spring supplied with H2-bearing gases; rapid cooling or dilution helps suppress over-reduction to polyols\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"H2 activated on Fe-Ni sulfide surfaces reduces one aldehyde group of glyoxal to an alcohol, giving the hydroxyaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This provides a second entry to glycolaldehyde without invoking the much weaker direct homologation of CO. The step is more condition-sensitive than formose initiation, but partial reduction of a dialdehyde on reduced metal sulfide surfaces in H2-bearing geothermal settings is chemically plausible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"One-pot Strecker synthesis of serine nitrile from glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"mineral-lined pore or subsurface portion of an evaporitic pool; mildly basic pH so part of the HCN inventory is present as CN-; intermittent drying raises reagent activities\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycolaldehyde condenses with ammonia to an imine in situ, followed by cyanide addition to give the aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolaldehyde is the correct aldehyde precursor for serine. The Strecker manifold is among the most direct prebiotic routes to alpha-amino acid precursors, and surface concentration by evaporation or adsorption can compensate for the reversible imine-forming step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Stepwise imine formation from glycolaldehyde and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"evaporative pool margin, clay film, or silica-rich hot-spring apron; dehydration favored during drying phases\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of the aldehyde with ammonia yields the hydroxy-imine with loss of water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This makes the imine reservoir explicit instead of hiding it inside a one-pot Strecker step. Although transient, such imines are chemically expected under concentration-dehydration cycles and provide a defensible branching point for cyanide capture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide addition to glycolaldehyde imine to form serine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanide-bearing pool at mildly basic pH; mineral adsorption can protect the imine long enough for capture\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CN- adds nucleophilically to the imine carbon to furnish the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the second half of the Strecker route written explicitly. Separating dehydration and cyanide addition gives a realistic alternative to the one-pot version under cycling surface conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Net hydrolysis of serine nitrile to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"long-lived geothermal pool with repeated heating and pH cycling; net transformation represents full nitrile hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the aminonitrile to the amino acid, releasing ammonia overall.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a mass-balanced net hydrolysis appropriate for prolonged aqueous heating. Because full nitrile hydrolysis is slow, the network also includes the more explicit amide-mediated sequence as a parallel branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of serine nitrile to serinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"geothermal pool or mineral interface; partial hydrolysis favored before full conversion\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water adds across the nitrile to form the corresponding primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Amide formation is the chemically expected first persistent intermediate during aminonitrile hydrolysis, making this route more realistic than assuming immediate full conversion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of serinamide to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"continued aqueous heating in a hot-spring pool; mildly acidic or mildly alkaline episodes accelerate turnover\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis gives the carboxylic acid, releasing ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the stepwise hydrolysis sequence and provides a mechanistically clearer endgame from the aminonitrile.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type formation of serine hydantoin from glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"carbonate-bearing evaporative pool; mildly basic pH so part of the HCN inventory is present as CN-; evaporation concentrates ammonium bicarbonate\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycolaldehyde enters cyanohydrin and imine chemistry with cyanide and ammonium bicarbonate, followed by cyclization to the hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Bucherer-Bergs manifold provides a protected-precursor route from aldehydes to amino acids. Using ammonium bicarbonate as an explicit feedstock makes the required carbonate-ammonium speciation clear and avoids an under-defined free-CO2 formulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic ring opening of serine hydantoin to N-carbamylserine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"heated aqueous pool with prolonged residence time\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water opens the hydantoin ring to the corresponding N-carbamyl amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the more realistic first hydrolytic fate of hydantoin derivatives; it avoids collapsing a protected precursor directly to the free amino acid in a single step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis and decarbamylation of N-carbamylserine to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"continued geothermal cycling or hot-spring heating\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The carbamyl group hydrolyzes and decomposes, yielding serine with release of carbon dioxide and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the protected hydantoin route under forcing aqueous conditions. It is slower than the nitrile endgame but offers a plausible reservoir-based alternative in which the amino acid is liberated only after ring opening and decarbamylation.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose-Strecker-direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \